ef 1075 #edb/ref 17592186352966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354108 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354108 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354108 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186352849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354108 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a6620bf0-0854-567b-ab31-b12fdbf4f589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354309 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354309 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84607" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354309 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354309 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ 1 & 0 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the third column of the matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186354309 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ad91747-58ce-5cb7-bde7-896fa9da6f06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354309 #edb/ref 1074 "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nSo, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{3}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations whose augmented matrix $M$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix is already  in row echelon form.  It has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.  From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}(-2(\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3})-3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186354753 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354753 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354753 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "098be5a2-3449-53d3-8787-924e2faef4a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354765 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354765 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354765 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ae5ca6c-b6ab-5388-877b-d5ca6d9eb62b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354967 #edb/ref 1080 "\\pi \\,\\text{s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354967 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354967 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2421dbf5-639c-51c5-ac36-217830997498" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355493 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355493 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355493 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5d972e39-e927-56e2-b99d-4e46df